WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.860
Dangling plot threads and dumb decisions...&nbsp;
"Avatar: The Way of Water" is gorgeous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.340
epic, and moving, but these plot&nbsp;
holes prove it isn't perfect.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.140
After the return of Colonel Quaritch, Jake&nbsp;
and his family decide to go into hiding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.620
protect the Omaticaya clan from the&nbsp;
Colonel's wrath. Since the Colonel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.580
only wants revenge on his human form's&nbsp;
killer, Jake assumes Quaritch will leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.000
the Omaticaya alone. But going into&nbsp;
hiding doesn't guarantee anything.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.140
The plan hinges on the assumption that&nbsp;
Quaritch knows what Jake knows. Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.860
didn't exactly announce to the Resource&nbsp;
Development Administration — a.k.a. the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.640
RDA — that he'd be leaving the jungle.&nbsp;
Quaritch has no reason to believe Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.460
and his family would leave their&nbsp;
home in the Hallelujah Mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.840
where they'd be mostly safe thanks to the&nbsp;
Flux Vortex and the Omaticaya's guerilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.280
warfare tactics. It follows that he would try&nbsp;
to find Jake in the Hallelujah Mountains first.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.000
Even if Quaritch couldn't seize&nbsp;
the Hallelujah Mountains, he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.200
certainly be capable of taking individual&nbsp;
Na'vi prisoners, like he already did with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.060
Spider and almost did with Jake's children. If&nbsp;
the Omaticaya tried to explain that Jake fled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.440
Quaritch might not believe them. After all,&nbsp;
he didn't believe the innocent Metkayina&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.220
tribes when they told him they hadn't seen&nbsp;
Jake, and he still torched their homes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.600
It was only by a stroke of luck that the&nbsp;
Omaticaya tribe was spared the same fate.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.920
Jake has a huge bounty on his head in&nbsp;
"The Way of Water," but the plot may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.040
overstating his importance. Does&nbsp;
the RDA really think that taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.040
out a single person will silence&nbsp;
the Na'vi insurrection for good?

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.080
It makes sense for Quaritch to be single-mindedly&nbsp;
focused on finding Jake. He doesn't care about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.640
other Na'vi; he just wants revenge. But Quaritch&nbsp;
isn't the only one making decisions at the RDA.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.420
Certainly, killing Jake would deal a&nbsp;
huge blow to the resistance. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.000
the one who rallied the Na'vi&nbsp;
to victory fifteen years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.560
and without him it would take them a long&nbsp;
time to regroup. Jake is also one of the few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:48.180
characters on Pandora who has inside knowledge&nbsp;
of the RDA. But he's not the only one who knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.080
how to use human technology, and he's not&nbsp;
the only one capable of organizing an army.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.580
This oversight is especially apparent in&nbsp;
the battle at sea. Viewers will notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.200
Tonowari gathers his clan into an imposing&nbsp;
battle formation — and on very short notice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.100
too. This moment reminds audiences that&nbsp;
the Na'vi are quite capable of organizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.900
themselves. Clearly, the movement to free&nbsp;
Pandora will live on even if Jake dies. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:13.620
maybe the last thing the RDA wants&nbsp;
is to turn Jake into a martyr.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.640
When they learn that their kids are being&nbsp;
held hostage in the forest, Jake and Neytiri&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.060
swoop in to rescue them. It's a good thing&nbsp;
that they're able to respond so quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.200
but it's a little surprising that their rescue&nbsp;
party consists of only two people. Why didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.640
they ask their clan for reinforcements? Perhaps&nbsp;
with backup they could have prevented Spider's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.840
capture. You'd think Jake and Neytiri would&nbsp;
pull out all the stops to save their children.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.660
Jake and his son were both&nbsp;
equipped with throat mics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.380
so it should have been relatively easy&nbsp;
for Jake to radio for help. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.580
it's not like Jake and his family are the only&nbsp;
Na'Vi using human technology. Considering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.840
Jake and the Omaticaya clan have been staging&nbsp;
guerrilla warfare against the RDA for the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.200
year, it's hard to believe that all the&nbsp;
Na'Vi aren't equipped with throat mics.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.220
While there may not be an in-story reason&nbsp;
for this, the choice at least makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.840
from a storytelling perspective. Most likely, the&nbsp;
filmmakers wanted to keep the cast to a manageable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.980
size, and that meant not dragging a bunch of&nbsp;
additional characters into the fight. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.360
is about Jake and his family, so it makes sense&nbsp;
that only these characters would be involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:15.060
If nothing else, this is one movie epic in scale,&nbsp;
but maintaining an intimate focus. Still, no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.140
some viewers were frustrated watching this scene,&nbsp;
knowing that Jake could have made a smarter move.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.640
Without unobtanium, the events of "Avatar"&nbsp;
would have never happened. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.160
it was the whole reason the RDA&nbsp;
funded the mission on Pandora.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.540
"Because this little gray rock sells for&nbsp;
$20 million a kilo. That's the only reason."

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:34.680
Yet, unobtanium seems to be the last&nbsp;
thing on the RDA's mind these days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.480
General Ardmore insists that the company's&nbsp;
new objective is colonization, not mining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.700
because Earth is pretty much&nbsp;
a lost cause at this point.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.920
It's understandable why the RDA's priorities might&nbsp;
have changed. Fifteen years have passed on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.780
more than enough time for war and climate change&nbsp;
to render it nearly unlivable. Perhaps all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.560
unobtanium in the world won't do them any&nbsp;
good, because soon everyone on the planet is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.180
going to need to jump ship. Still, it's a little&nbsp;
strange that the film never mentions unobtanium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.020
not even once. You would think that the&nbsp;
miraculous material would come in handy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:07.860
for the RDA if they want to expand their&nbsp;
colony and crush the Na'vi resistance.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:13.440
The RDA's purpose is further obfuscated in the&nbsp;
scene where tulkun hunter Mick Scoresby extracts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.840
a chemical called amrita from the tulkun's brain.&nbsp;
Scoresby brags that amrita can effectively stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.240
aging entirely, making it eclipse unobtanium as&nbsp;
the most valuable resource on Pandora. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.200
adds that amrita is "paying for everything on&nbsp;
Pandora," which seems to contradict the notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.240
that the RDA cares only about colonization. While&nbsp;
it makes sense that amrita is extremely valuable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:37.560
it seems doubtful it would be of much use&nbsp;
to the humans back on a dying planet Earth.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.480
Colonel Quaritch goes to some great lengths&nbsp;
to bait Jake and capture his children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.840
even though he already has one of&nbsp;
Jake's children in his custody. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.040
Spider is technically Jake's adoptive son,&nbsp;
but Jake still cares about the boy. Why lay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.820
out an elaborate trap for Jake when the&nbsp;
RDA already has something Jake wants?

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.980
Obviously, the real reason Quaritch doesn't hold&nbsp;
Spider hostage is clear: Spider is his son. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.180
as one key scene from the climax demonstrates,&nbsp;
Quaritch loves his son more than he hates Jake.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.900
"I'm getting all emotional. Might&nbsp;
just give you a big wet kiss."

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.340
Yet, once again, Quaritch isn't the one in&nbsp;
charge, and it seems like General Ardmore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:14.760
has no such qualms about using Spider&nbsp;
that way. Why doesn't she just order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.620
Quaritch to ransom his son? The only reason&nbsp;
why the RDA might not take advantage of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.040
prisoner they already had would be if&nbsp;
Quaritch was convinced that Spider was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.180
genuinely cooperating with his captors. But&nbsp;
General Ardmore would need to be convinced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.320
too, and somehow it seems doubtful she would&nbsp;
automatically trust the boy like Quaritch.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.060
"Avatar: The Way of Water" isn't the first movie&nbsp;
in the "Avatar" franchise to contradict itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.260
about the science of Pandora. The first film made&nbsp;
it clear that humans couldn't survive long without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.820
a mask in Pandora's atmosphere; specifically,&nbsp;
they'd be unconscious after 20 seconds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.200
dead after 4 minutes. But that doesn't seem to&nbsp;
stop Quaritch from opening an airlock and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.940
firing on Jake for a heck of a lot longer than&nbsp;
20 seconds. Though, granted, he's basically a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.180
finely tuned murder machine and would have&nbsp;
no trouble holding his breath that long.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.860
But the sequel also plays a little fast and&nbsp;
loose with the specifics. Early in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:08.340
Spider establishes a key rule of&nbsp;
worldbuilding. He tells the Na'vi,

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.060
"You can breathe Earth air for hours. I can&nbsp;
only breathe yours for, like, 10 seconds."

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:18.180
Yet Quaritch, who now inhabits a Na'vi avatar with&nbsp;
recombinant DNA – "recom" for short – doesn't seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:21.480
to follow this rule. Quaritch is quite&nbsp;
comfortable breathing Pandora's air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.460
as well as the Earth air inside the RDA compounds,&nbsp;
aside from a brief moment when Quaritch sucks in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:31.320
a few gulps from a gas mask before talking to&nbsp;
Spider. One possible explanation here is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:36.120
the recoms were bred to be able to breathe in both&nbsp;
Earth's atmosphere and that of Pandora — though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.760
it might have been nice if the film&nbsp;
had taken a moment to mention that.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.460
"Just breathe. Breathe."

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:45.900
To be honest, Quaritch attempting to ride&nbsp;
a Banshee wasn't the smartest move. Surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.560
Quaritch could tell that Spider was hoping&nbsp;
the Colonel would humiliate himself. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:53.100
that's not a plot hole in itself, because it&nbsp;
makes perfect sense for Quaritch's character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.580
He's stubborn and wants to prove he can&nbsp;
do anything that Jake can do, so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.900
why he takes the bait. However, one moment&nbsp;
from the Banshee scene doesn't quite add up.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.160
Quaritch is seen tumbling off the cliff on the&nbsp;
back of a Banshee much like Jake did in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.080
first film, only to emerge victorious a minute&nbsp;
later. How does he learn so quickly? Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.160
the Colonel is a novice to Banshee-riding,&nbsp;
not even knowing that he should muzzle the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:17.880
Banshee before trying to mount it. According&nbsp;
to the rules established in the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.080
Quaritch could have only tamed the beast by&nbsp;
completing the bond which involves linking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.760
the nerve receptors in his queue to the Banshee's&nbsp;
receptors. But how does Quaritch know about this?

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.860
Most likely, the RDA knows about this ritual from&nbsp;
Jake's videologs from the first movie, but whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:36.060
or not Quaritch knows is a different story.&nbsp;
Knowing Quaritch, he wouldn't do his homework.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.200
He has a pretty rudimentary grasp of the Na'vi&nbsp;
language, to the point that Spider says he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:45.120
the vocabulary of a three-year-old. These Avatar&nbsp;
movies sure do have a grudge against babies.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:46.753
"You're like a baby."

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.060
Anyway, the point is the Colonel would&nbsp;
have probably only researched the bare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:54.360
minimum necessary for the mission. And&nbsp;
unless he predicted that his guide would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.860
goad him into trying to tame a Banshee, the&nbsp;
bonding ritual probably wasn't in his plans.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.900
The first half of "The Way of Water" seems to be&nbsp;
building toward Quaritch and his fellow recoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.340
trying to go undercover among the Na'vi. That's&nbsp;
why they go out of their way to tame Banshees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:12.360
learn the language — so they can get close enough&nbsp;
to Jake to kill him, and then destroy the Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.560
insurrection from the inside. Yet, this plot&nbsp;
thread is abandoned halfway through the movie.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.400
It's understandable why the filmmakers&nbsp;
didn't take this route. The "undercover"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.240
angle was already explored in the first&nbsp;
movie with Jake, and honestly the recoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.140
wouldn't have gotten very far — they wouldn't&nbsp;
fool a real Na'vi for an instant. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:32.760
the way that Quaritch and his team switch gears&nbsp;
is a little contrived: the RDA conveniently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.300
picks up a signal from a rogue gunship,&nbsp;
which sends the plot in a new direction.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.300
Everything that happens in the&nbsp;
second half of the movie hinges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.420
on Quaritch guessing — correctly&nbsp;
— that the unidentified aircraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.160
will lead him straight to Jake. That's an&nbsp;
awfully lucky guess. Up until this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.540
Quaritch had every reason to believe that Jake&nbsp;
was in the Hallelujah Mountains. Besides, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:56.400
feels implausible that General Ardmore would let&nbsp;
Quaritch travel halfway around Pandora on a hunch.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.020
There's one plot hole in "Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water" so enormous that Reddit users called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.520
it out almost immediately after the film came&nbsp;
out: Where is Jake's army when he needs it most?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.140
The Metkayina clan had Jake's back throughout&nbsp;
the first hostage exchange on the ship and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:14.940
ensuing battle — but the moment that the chief's&nbsp;
daughter Tsireya is rescued, the clan is nowhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.320
to be found, leaving Jake and his family to&nbsp;
fend for themselves. So, why did they bail?

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.220
Perhaps the Metkayina retreated after&nbsp;
Tsireya was safe because they no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.120
had a personal stake in the conflict. Or&nbsp;
maybe they were physically unable to help.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.620
One Reddit user speculated,

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.480
"They couldn't get past the fire on the water."

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:34.440
That is a fair point, though it seems like&nbsp;
the Metkayina might have been able to swim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.000
under. Meanwhile, another Redditor offered&nbsp;
a solid explanation — even if it wasn't an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.620
in-universe explanation — saying that the real&nbsp;
focus of this part was the family unit. Had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.700
filmmakers included the whole army in the final&nbsp;
battle, it would have become too crowded. Trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:52.260
to focus on any characters other than Jake and his&nbsp;
family would, honestly, have only hurt the story.

